# autofleet, driving itself.
#
# This repo is its own reference installation: the framework in scripts/fleet/
# is the framework a host project vendors, and these are the knobs that repo
# would set. Everything not named here keeps the default in
# scripts/fleet/config.sh.

# autofleet is shell and python. There is nothing to compile, no service to
# stand up, and no port to hand out -- so no compose file, no ports, and no
# project setup hook beyond the one below.
AUTOFLEET_PROJECT_PREFIX=autofleet
AUTOFLEET_TEST_COMMAND="./tests/run.sh"
AUTOFLEET_NOTIFY_TITLE="autofleet"

# TWO WORKTREES, NOT THREE, while what an issue costs is still being brought
# down. Parallelism does not reduce what the backlog spends -- it spends it
# faster, three issues at a time, which is how one day took a week's budget.
# Measured here: issue #71 cost 207M tokens and #13 196M, against #106's 10.5M
# for comparable work. Two halves the blast radius of one of those and leaves a
# chance of noticing it before the others have finished too. Back to three once
# the per-issue numbers hold under 20M.
AUTOFLEET_MAX=2


# WHAT ONE BUILD MAY SPEND, left at the payload defaults for now.
#
# A knob called AUTOFLEET_CONTEXT_RECYCLE lived here and was set to 2700 -- drop
# the build's conversation every 45 minutes, because issue #71's build ran 285
# turns in ONE window at 229,052 cache-read tokens a turn (65M, 31.9% of a 207M
# issue) against issue #106's 61 turns at 96,061 for comparable work. The cost
# was never the turn count: every turn re-sends everything the session has read,
# so a long build pays for its whole history again on each turn of it.
#
# armaatus/autofleet#151 removed the cause rather than the symptom. A build is
# one `claude -p` that ENDS -- at AUTOFLEET_BUILD_MAX_TURNS or at
# AUTOFLEET_BUILD_MAX_BUDGET_USD -- and a second run starts from the branch and
# the pull request, which is state no session holds. So there is nothing to
# recycle and nothing to hand over.
#
# THE NUMBERS ARE A FIRST GUESS, exactly as 2700 was, and this is the repository
# where the measurement came from -- so the thing to do after the next run is
# compare `fleet.sh cost` against the 207M/10.5M figures above and move them.
# They are the payload defaults (400 turns, $25) until then.

# ONE REVIEW, ONE FIX, AND THE KNOBS ARE THE PAYLOAD DEFAULTS.
#
# Five knobs used to live here -- a review mode, a delta scope, and the numbers
# bounding a loop of one review and up to two validations. armaatus/autofleet#152
# removed the loop: the reviewer answers with a JSON verdict, a `request-changes`
# buys exactly one fix session, and the re-review of that fix is final. There is
# no mode to choose, because `.github/workflows/claude-review.yml` is gone and
# the dispatcher is the only venue; there is no round two to take a delta of.
#
# What this repository still gives up by reviewing itself is unchanged and is
# worth knowing: there is no CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN here, so the reviewer signs
# in as the account that opened the pull request. GitHub refuses `--approve` on
# your own PR, so the verdict arrives as a COMMENTED review carrying the marker
# `merge_gate.py` reads. docs/CONFIGURATION.md has the row.

# EVERY FLEET-OWNED CLAUDE IS OPUS. Without these, `claude -p` inherits the
# model from ~/.claude/settings.json -- the maintainer's interactive choice --
# and a build ran on Fable on 2026-09-23 for that reason. The wrapper is one
# word so review.sh's quoted invocation of the seam still works; $REPO_ROOT is
# set by config.sh before this file is sourced.
AUTOFLEET_BUILD_CMD="$REPO_ROOT/.autofleet/claude-opus"
AUTOFLEET_REVIEW_CMD="$REPO_ROOT/.autofleet/claude-opus"
